<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jarawa (Berbers)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jarawa were a Zenata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a> mountains in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in the 7th century. Under their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dihya">Dihya</a>, called al-Kahina, they led Berber resistance to the Umayyad conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 690s. Some medieval Arab writers describe them as practising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, a claim that historians treat with caution.</p></div>
